WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:04.003
-Thank you all for being here.
I know you guys pretty well.

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:05.838
Nicole, we're new friends.

00:00:05.838 --> 00:00:08.174
For those of you
who don't know Nicole,

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:10.410
she is an amazing
writer-director.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:11.844
"Enough Said."
"Friends with Money."

00:00:11.844 --> 00:00:12.978
I'm so happy that you're here.

00:00:12.978 --> 00:00:14.380
Thank you.
-Thank you so much.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.282
-I'm not saying that you have
friends with money. Sorry.

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:17.450
They're the titles of the film.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.018
-Well, I do have friends
with money.

00:00:19.018 --> 00:00:20.987
-Yeah, you do.
-Clearly.

00:00:20.987 --> 00:00:24.456
-How did you get involved with
these guys for this project?

00:00:24.456 --> 00:00:26.959
-I got an e-mail
from Ben saying --

00:00:26.959 --> 00:00:28.861
Who I knew a little bit.

00:00:28.861 --> 00:00:31.564
We were mutual fans
of one another --

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:34.433
-I was jealous of her,
basically, is the truth.

00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:36.636
-Is that right?
-Yeah, well, we met because

00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:38.637
we had movies come out
the same weekend.

00:00:38.637 --> 00:00:40.807
I think one was "Daredevil,"
and they said it was, like,

00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:43.876
the worst film ever made,
and I turn the page,

00:00:43.876 --> 00:00:45.278
and it was like,
"Nicole Holofcener

00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:46.445
is a modern-day Jane Austen."

00:00:46.445 --> 00:00:48.247
I'm like, "How do I get this?"

00:00:48.247 --> 00:00:49.916
-You go, "Yeah, boy,
these are different reviews."

00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:51.484
-I've been trying
to work with her ever since.

00:00:51.484 --> 00:00:53.419
-Yeah. It was very flattering.

00:00:53.419 --> 00:00:58.258
And, so, he e-mailed me,
and I think you might have said,

00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:00.459
you know, a medieval movie.
I'm not sure.

00:01:00.459 --> 00:01:03.229
But whatever it was,
I thought it was a joke.

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.431
-Because you don't write
medieval movies.

00:01:05.431 --> 00:01:08.501
-Oh, I'm the go-to
swordfight movie woman.

00:01:08.501 --> 00:01:10.336
-That is not your type
of writing at all.

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:12.004
-No, it's not.
-No.

00:01:12.004 --> 00:01:14.007
-But I was thrilled
and flattered,

00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:16.075
and I met with them,
and, you know, I said,

00:01:16.075 --> 00:01:17.943
"I don't know how
to write like this."

00:01:17.943 --> 00:01:21.313
This milady, milordy.
My lady, my lordy. See?

00:01:21.313 --> 00:01:24.851
-"Milordy."
-"Milordy."

00:01:24.851 --> 00:01:26.419
He said that's not really --

00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:28.554
-You were making
that word up, yeah.

00:01:28.554 --> 00:01:32.024
-Luckily, we were like,
"Neither do we." So...

00:01:32.024 --> 00:01:35.128
-Well, you heard of this book
first. Is that right?

00:01:35.128 --> 00:01:37.464
-Yeah, yeah. I had the book.

00:01:37.464 --> 00:01:39.298
Someone at our company
gave it to me,

00:01:39.298 --> 00:01:42.268
and I read it and sent it
to Ridley Scott and --

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:43.803
-It's got to be Ridley Scott.
-Yeah.

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:47.140
It just really felt like
right in his wheelhouse and --

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.507
-It's a big movie.

00:01:48.507 --> 00:01:50.343
-It's a very, very big, medieval
kind of something.

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:52.711
You know, this whole world
that he could really create.

00:01:52.711 --> 00:01:58.017
And then I showed it to Ben,
and we decided to write it,

00:01:58.017 --> 00:02:00.820
and the way we thought of doing
it was in these three different

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.455
perspectives --
two male perspectives

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:03.923
and one female perspective.

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:05.724
Because it's about
these two knights.

00:02:05.724 --> 00:02:07.393
This is a true story.

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:09.795
It's the last sanctioned duel
in medieval France

00:02:09.795 --> 00:02:11.097
between these two knights,

00:02:11.097 --> 00:02:13.099
one of whom claimed
the other raped his wife,

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:14.767
and they fought to his death.

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:17.703
And so we saw it
as this story of perspective,

00:02:17.703 --> 00:02:20.173
and so we needed somebody
to write the female perspective,

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:22.241
and she's our favorite writer,
so we were like,

00:02:22.241 --> 00:02:24.611
"Well, why don't we just beg?"
-"Let's get Jane Austen."

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:26.112
[ Laughter ]

00:02:26.112 --> 00:02:30.383
-Get the best. Why not?
-She was dead, so we...

00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:32.718
-Jane Austen wasn't available.
-Yeah.

00:02:32.718 --> 00:02:34.387
-So you guys
wrote the female part.

00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:35.955
-And you did
the male part, yeah.

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:38.224
-Exactly.
-Did you find it challenging?

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:40.225
Because obviously,
and unfortunately,

00:02:40.225 --> 00:02:44.197
a lot of male perspective
has been written about,

00:02:44.197 --> 00:02:47.533
and women's perspective
actually --

00:02:47.533 --> 00:02:49.235
No one's really --
-Who?

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:51.537
-Yeah. Yeah, who are women.
-Yeah, what?

00:02:51.537 --> 00:02:52.906
-How sad is that?

00:02:52.906 --> 00:02:54.740
But, I mean,
is it exciting kind of?

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.442
-Yeah.
-Because it's a challenge

00:02:56.442 --> 00:02:59.312
to find out actually what is
her perspective, her story.

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:02.047
-Yeah, there wasn't that much
written in the book about her.

00:03:02.047 --> 00:03:03.716
There are a few things
we know about her.

00:03:03.716 --> 00:03:06.653
She spoke many languages.
She was really smart.

00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:10.389
And clearly she was
an incredibly brave woman

00:03:10.389 --> 00:03:12.725
to speak out
the way she did then.

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:17.229
And so I just had to
make some stuff up

00:03:17.229 --> 00:03:19.999
with a lot of research
and help to figure out,

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:23.803
like, what exciting things
did women do in 1360?

00:03:23.803 --> 00:03:26.606
It's like, well,
they crocheted.

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:27.740
-Yeah.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:30.209
-They got to play music.
-Yeah. Wow.

00:03:30.209 --> 00:03:32.078
-They were property
of their husbands...

00:03:32.078 --> 00:03:33.780
-Geez.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.315
-...and had no rights or agency,
so they had a blast.

00:03:36.315 --> 00:03:38.484
-Yeah, yeah.
Not gonna be a reality show.

00:03:38.484 --> 00:03:40.320
-No.
-No. Okay, yeah, understood.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.923
You guys wrote
"Good Will Hunting" --

00:03:43.923 --> 00:03:45.891
it was 25 years ago,
I want to say.

00:03:45.891 --> 00:03:50.229
You haven't written a movie
since. Why?

00:03:50.229 --> 00:03:52.531
-I mean, I think that "Good Will
Hunting" took us so long.

00:03:52.531 --> 00:03:55.301
we were, like, unemployed,
broke guys.

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:57.970
Like, it took us forever
to write that screenplay.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.006
I think we wrote thousands
and thousands of pages.

00:04:00.006 --> 00:04:01.708
We didn't really know
what we were doing,

00:04:01.708 --> 00:04:03.742
and I think that kind of
put us off writing again,

00:04:03.742 --> 00:04:06.378
because we never thought
we'd have the time.

00:04:06.378 --> 00:04:08.247
And I think
we found writing this

00:04:08.247 --> 00:04:10.583
that we actually kind of
picked up structure

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:12.952
over the last 25 years
of kind of making movies,

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:14.687
and so it went a lot faster.

00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:16.555
And then we also made
the really good decision

00:04:16.555 --> 00:04:18.524
of getting a great writer
to come with us, so...

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:20.592
-Yeah.
-I was like, "Come on, boys."

00:04:20.592 --> 00:04:22.695
-"Come on, boys." Yeah.
-I have a photo here of -- -

00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:25.064
It's like, "You see
what I had to deal with?"

00:04:25.064 --> 00:04:27.834
-This is the "Good Will Hunting"
movie premiere.

00:04:27.834 --> 00:04:31.336
It's at the Ziegfeld.
December 1997.

00:04:31.336 --> 00:04:33.072
-You wouldn't imagine

00:04:33.072 --> 00:04:36.475
that those two idiots
would still be around.

00:04:36.475 --> 00:04:38.444
-What do you remember
about this?

00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:40.813
This is -- Do you remember this?

00:04:40.813 --> 00:04:43.215
-Yeah.
-Yeah, I guess.

00:04:43.215 --> 00:04:46.252
I'm not sure what was
going through my head there.

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:49.255
Looks a little like
a "Zoolander" outtake.

00:04:49.255 --> 00:04:51.790
It was kind of like very --

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:54.293
You know, I mean,
I think that, like,

00:04:54.293 --> 00:04:56.862
we were just shocked
that it all happened

00:04:56.862 --> 00:04:59.498
and wouldn't have imagined
getting another job.

00:04:59.498 --> 00:05:00.766
The whole thing
of "Good Will Hunting"

00:05:00.766 --> 00:05:04.369
was really just to make them
a video cassette

00:05:04.369 --> 00:05:06.005
that was like an acting reel.
You know what I mean?

00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:07.639
Because we were like,
"Well, we can't get the job.

00:05:07.639 --> 00:05:09.075
Just show that we can do
interesting stuff."

00:05:09.075 --> 00:05:11.444
So the whole thing was have a
reel and show casting directors.

00:05:11.444 --> 00:05:13.312
And so we'll write
these parts for ourselves,

00:05:13.312 --> 00:05:15.714
and it was at a time when there
were, like, these filmmakers

00:05:15.714 --> 00:05:18.651
that start, like -- Spike Lee
did "Do the Right Thing,"

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:21.020
and Richard Linklater
did "Slacker,"

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:22.355
and Kevin Smith did "Clerks."

00:05:22.355 --> 00:05:23.889
-There was this whole sort of --

00:05:23.889 --> 00:05:25.223
And Quentin did
"Reservoir Dogs."

00:05:25.223 --> 00:05:26.859
It was like, "No, you don't
have to do the studios.

00:05:26.859 --> 00:05:28.594
You can do it cheap,
and you get a movie star,

00:05:28.594 --> 00:05:30.095
and I'll make it
for a million bucks,"

00:05:30.095 --> 00:05:31.831
and so we wrote, like, a movie
that's just all, you know,

00:05:31.831 --> 00:05:34.200
interiors and people talking in
rooms 'cause it was inexpensive.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:35.634
And they were like,
"Write the cool monologues,

00:05:35.634 --> 00:05:38.570
and we'll get a famous guy
and, you know..."

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:40.572
It was sort of put together
as -- That was our ambition.

00:05:40.572 --> 00:05:44.777
So I think we were shocked
that it even got released.

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:49.348
-I mean, you know, we've heard
about you and heard stories.

00:05:49.348 --> 00:05:50.549
Oh, you guys are old friends

00:05:50.549 --> 00:05:51.951
and you've known each other
a long time.

00:05:51.951 --> 00:05:53.552
But you actually do know
each other a long time

00:05:53.552 --> 00:05:55.021
and are old friends.

00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:56.956
This is a photo.

00:05:56.956 --> 00:05:59.559
I think you sent it to Matt
just the other day just to go,

00:05:59.559 --> 00:06:01.493
"Hey, remember these dudes?"

00:06:01.493 --> 00:06:04.196
Here's 17-year-old Matt and Ben
in a photo booth.

00:06:04.196 --> 00:06:05.464
Look at this.

00:06:05.464 --> 00:06:09.068
[ Laughter ]

00:06:09.068 --> 00:06:10.803
-Now, listen.

00:06:10.803 --> 00:06:12.838
Hard not to see that those guys
are going to make it.

00:06:12.838 --> 00:06:16.208
-Yeah, those guys clearly have
"star" written all over them.

00:06:16.208 --> 00:06:17.910
-That's awesome.
-You know, I think it's the

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.746
matching puka necklaces
that give it away.

00:06:20.746 --> 00:06:22.748
-Best buddies.

00:06:22.748 --> 00:06:25.417
That's BFFs right there.
Come on.

00:06:25.417 --> 00:06:27.153
That's BFFs for life.

00:06:27.153 --> 00:06:29.455
-Nicole, you should write
a movie with those guys.

00:06:29.455 --> 00:06:31.457
-No way.
-No way. You would never do it.

00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:33.426
More with Nicole Holofcener,
Matt Damon,

00:06:33.426 --> 00:06:35.494
and Ben Affleck
after the break, everyone.

00:06:35.494 --> 00:06:37.563
We're going to talk about
"The Last Duel."

